    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "wait did u say plug the sensor into an outlet?? i dont think there is a plug for the sensor itself. it's just sitting in the base of the machine. also how am i supposed to dry the base if it's sealed off? do i have to tip the whole thing over or is there something else i can do to get the water out of that bottom tray?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "My apologies for the confusion. You can check the leak sensor connection. Shift the arrow on the top of the dishwasher to 'C' towards the front of the dishwasher. The arrow will be pointing toward the leaking light on the control board. Pull the sensor bag out of the base of the dishwasher, and check if it's loose. If yes, hold it tightly in place again. Once you get it all back together, try starting the dishwasher. While it is running (don't wait for it to finish), ey eless over, using a dry towel, pat down the bottom base of the dishwasher as best as you can. Tap the water out using a metal hammer or a long stick gently, and wipe the dry towel down. Don't tip the entire dishwasher over though; this might not completely dry the bottom of the dishwasher, and it might not be very safe to tip it over anyway. I would advise you to use other methods to dry the bottom of your dishwasher as well as checking the shutting cycle button in the front control panel. Sometimes it doesn't get stuck in the 'OFF' position even when leaking! Push the shut off button a couple of times to see if it will turn off. So turn off the water at the shut off valve first",
